WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.220
Merry Christmas!

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.320
90% of Americans in a November

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.240
Monmouth University poll say they plan
to celebrate Christmas, down slightly

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.389
from 96 percent who gave that response
in a similar Gallup poll a quarter

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:20.400
century ago. In a 2017 poll, the Pew
Research Center suggested that the

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.630
nature of Christmas may be changing,
driven in part by generational change. In

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.099
the poll, majorities of all age groups
felt that religious aspects of Christmas

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:33.120
were emphasized less than in the past,
but older people were bothered more by

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.980
this than younger ones. There is other
evidence of changing attitudes. 42% of

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.190
millennials,
compared to 58% of boomers, say

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.390
they plan to attend religious services
on Christmas Eve or Christmas day. 6

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.620
in 10 millennials, compared to 46
percent of boomers, say it doesn't matter

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.739
to them whether merchants say "Merry
Christmas" or "Happy Holidays".

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:57.690
Older people prefer "Merry Christmas".
Politics aside, more Americans find the

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.170
holiday season more enjoyable than
stressful compared to the rest of the

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.720
year, and around 8 in 10 planned to
gather with family and friends on Christmas.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.540
Are you celebrating Christmas?
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:15.840
what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.770
sure to LIKE and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

